---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Hero from '../components/Hero.astro';
import CampaignFigure from '../components/CampaignFigure.astro';
import Section from '../components/Section.astro';
import { delta, deltaCta, deltaNav, site } from '../config';

// Delta's page: the cockpit, the campaign, and the package. The campaign shape
// follows the contract in kletos/apps/delta, not a second model.
const declaration = `{
  "id": "emergency-lift",
  "title": "Emergency lift",
  "start": { "fact": "delta-emergency-active" },
  "end": { "fact": "treatment-started" },
  "objectives": [
    {
      "id": "scan-reported",
      "title": "the scan reaches the hospital",
      "when": { "fact": "scan-reported" },
      "within": 75
    }
  ]
}`;
---

<Base
  title={`${delta.name}: ${delta.title}`}
  description={delta.description}
  nav={deltaNav}
  cta={deltaCta}
>
  <Hero
    eyebrow="Delta"
    title={delta.tagline}
    lede="Delta runs a campaign, reads the record, and packages the result. A campaign is one run inside a project: a situation, a briefing, objectives, and the constraints that bound them. The package is the run, and it can be replayed and compared."
    body="The cockpit does not keep a second model of the work. It reads the record the project already holds, so an objective is met when the run says it is."
  >
    <CampaignFigure slot="figure" />
  </Hero>

  <Section
    id="cockpit"
    label="Cockpit"
    title="One reading of the run"
    lede="Delta shows the work as the record has it: the project, the claim, the bound, and the result."
  >
    <div class="cards cards--3">
      <div class="card">
        <span class="card__value">What is running</span>
        <p>The project and the campaign, with the state the record gives them.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <span class="card__value">Who claimed it</span>
        <p>The claim, the operator, and the authority that took the work.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <span class="card__value">What bounded it</span>
        <p>The hints and the terms that limited the run.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <p class="note">
      Scrub the run backwards and an objective returns to open. The cockpit cannot show a state
      the record does not support.
    </p>
  </Section>

  <Section
    id="campaigns"
    label="Campaigns"
    title="A campaign is data of the project"
    lede="A project is the container: its record, its place, its members, and its resources. A campaign is one run inside it, with a start, an end, and the sub-missions that make it up."
  >
    <dl class="spec">
      <dt>Start and end</dt>
      <dd>
        Conditions over the record, so a campaign begins when the run says it did, not at a scene
        boundary.
      </dd>
      <dt>An objective</dt>
      <dd>
        A condition over a fact, a hint, an intent, a count, or the run's clock. <code>all</code>,
        <code>any</code>, and <code>not</code> make it compound.
      </dd>
      <dt>A window</dt>
      <dd>
        <code>within</code> seconds after the start. The objective is read at the earlier of the
        cursor and the deadline, so a fact written after the window closes cannot rescue it.
      </dd>
      <dt>An optional objective</dt>
      <dd>A miss does not fail the campaign, and the HUD still shows it.</dd>
    </dl>

    <pre class="campaign-code"><code>{declaration}</code></pre>

    <p class="note">
      The contract refuses a condition it does not know rather than reading it as one that never
      fires.
    </p>
  </Section>

  <Section
    id="package"
    label="Package"
    title="The run becomes a package"
    lede="A finished campaign is a package. Ktema writes and addresses the record; Delta runs and reads the campaign; Kletos performs the work."
  >
    <dl class="spec">
      <dt>Situation</dt>
      <dd>The project, the place, and the facts that start the run.</dd>
      <dt>Briefing</dt>
      <dd>What the operator is told before the run.</dd>
      <dt>Requirements</dt>
      <dd>The objectives, their windows, and the constraints.</dd>
      <dt>Record</dt>
      <dd>Every fact, intent, claim, and hint, addressed and replayable.</dd>
      <dt>Result</dt>
      <dd>The campaign state, the objectives met, and the misses.</dd>
    </dl>

    <p class="note">
      The package travels. The record is self-describing and addressed by coordinate, so a run
      made on one machine reads on another with no conversion step. Two runs of the same package
      are read in the same coordinate space, so their objectives and records can be compared point
      by point.
    </p>
  </Section>

  <Section
    id="proof"
    label="Proof"
    title="The villa evacuation, in the working build"
    lede="The first campaign is an emergency lift on the Monaco coast. It starts when the emergency is declared and ends when treatment starts."
  >
    <ul class="metrics">
      <li class="metric">
        <span class="metric__value">1,164</span>
        <span class="metric__label">records in the run</span>
      </li>
      <li class="metric">
        <span class="metric__value">5</span>
        <span class="metric__label">objectives, one with a deadline</span>
      </li>
      <li class="metric">
        <span class="metric__value">75 s</span>
        <span class="metric__label">the scan has to reach the hospital</span>
      </li>
      <li class="metric">
        <span class="metric__value">3</span>
        <span class="metric__label">checks in the scenario</span>
      </li>
    </ul>

    <p class="note">
      The scan is written at 80 seconds, three seconds inside its window. The campaign line shows
      five of five met, and the record behind each one.
    </p>
  </Section>

  <Section
    id="contact"
    label="Contact"
    title="Bring a campaign to the cockpit."
    lede="We are choosing the first design partners. If you run a situation that has to be replayed and understood, write to us."
    band
  >
    <p><a class="btn btn--accent" href={`mailto:${site.contact}`}>Write to us</a></p>
  </Section>
</Base>

<style>
  .campaign-code {
    margin: 0;
    padding: 20px 22px;
    overflow-x: auto;
    border: 1px solid var(--line);
    background: var(--bg-2);
    color: var(--fg-2);
    font-family: var(--mono);
    font-size: 12.5px;
    line-height: 1.65;
  }

  .spec code {
    padding: 1px 5px;
    border: 1px solid var(--line);
    background: var(--bg);
    color: var(--fg);
    font-size: 0.86em;
  }


</style>
